A||||7||DJRice||V6 dies after driving thru water||||1||
Z||000000||DJRice||10-12-2000||11:14 PM||||Several weeks ago, my brother drove thru a large puddle of water in his 86 V6 and the engine died.  He had to let the car sit for 30-45 minutes and then it started fine.  I had this problem once in my 87 as well.  <P>Has anyone else had this problem?  Is this just a easy as water splashing up into the distributor?  I think that the distributor is a bit to high for this but Im not really sure.  Any thoughts or suggestions?<P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR>"You mean like the back seat of a Volkswagen?"||||reg||1
Z||000001||Standard||10-13-2000||01:05 AM||||It's usually the water shorting something out.  After the engine dries out it starts fine..  Or maybe it's the Fiero telling you not to drive through those deep puddles  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 151,000 miles and counting!         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">      <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/asthanos.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||My7Fieros||10-13-2000||01:08 AM||||I doubt its the distributor..Ive power washed my engines on all my Fieros..never had a single prob...does sound like something on the lower end shorting out.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Shiner||10-13-2000||01:39 AM||||"Any thoughts or suggestions?"<BR>Yeah, I got one.  Don't drive through large puddles!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||10-13-2000||08:56 AM||||Most times I've seen this happen it's the coil the plug wires or the distributor cap.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tigger||10-13-2000||10:32 AM||||Could have filled the starter motor with water making it short out or not work till it drained and dried out...  Only thing I can think of if the water level wasn't very deep.<P>------------------<BR>T-T-F-N, Tah Tah For Now!<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tigger.gif">           <BR>  <A HREF="mailto:RacingStripes@hotmail.com">RacingStripes@hotmail.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||DJRice||10-13-2000||11:38 AM||||It wasnt really deep water.  I thought for sure someone would step up with the same problem. <P>It happended to me once several years ago, and I havent had it happen since.  I had actually forgotten about it until I got the call from my little brother.  I guess the starter or solenoid could get soaked just right to short out for several minutes.<P><P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR>"You mean like the back seat of a Volkswagen?"||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||10-13-2000||05:15 PM||||doesn't have to be deep water to soak the ignition. The water gets sprayed everywhere with considerale force as you drive thru it.<P>I've had that problem before. changed cap and wires and never had it again. (Was years ago on my ford.) now I keep good wires on eveything... New wires went on my DIS motor within a week of getting the car. It's the first thing I change when people complain of this. <P>On HEI you also want to check the terminals for the module on the distributor and the coil primary connector. Those are suposed to have dielectric in them to keep water out. (DIS uses weather pack connectors but I still put some dielectic in them to make sure.)||||reg||1
